1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,176
A single CPU core ticks about four billion times per second.

2
00:00:04,176 --> 00:00:09,535
An NVIDIA H100 GPU does about a thousand trillion math operations per second.

3
00:00:09,535 --> 00:00:13,919
A Cerebras wafer moves twenty one petabytes of data per second.

4
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:16,355
Each of these is a processing unit,

5
00:00:16,355 --> 00:00:19,069
and each exists for a different reason.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:23,857
Here are nine of them, with real numbers:

7
00:00:23,857 --> 00:00:28,858
CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, LPU, wafer-scale engines,

8
00:00:28,858 --> 00:00:31,081
FPGA, ASIC, and DPU.

9
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,077
To compare them, we'll use one yardstick.

10
00:00:34,077 --> 00:00:36,042
When an AI model writes text,

11
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:40,784
every new word requires reading every weight of the model from memory.

12
00:00:40,784 --> 00:00:42,749
So the speed limit is simple:

13
00:00:42,749 --> 00:00:45,458
memory bandwidth, divided by model size.

14
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,056
Our test model: 8 billion parameters,

15
00:00:48,056 --> 00:00:52,831
stored at one byte each. That's 8 gigabytes to read for every single token.

16
00:00:53,667 --> 00:00:55,664
The CPU: a few dozen big,

17
00:00:55,664 --> 00:01:00,696
smart cores. A server chip like AMD's EPYC 9654 has 96 of them:

18
00:01:00,696 --> 00:01:03,572
twelve chiplets of eight cores each,

19
00:01:03,572 --> 00:01:06,049
running at up to 3.7 gigahertz.

20
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:10,822
Each core is built to finish one stream of instructions as fast as possible.

21
00:01:10,822 --> 00:01:13,160
It predicts which way branches will go,

22
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:17,595
runs instructions out of order, and keeps 384 megabytes of cache close by.

23
00:01:17,595 --> 00:01:19,333
That makes it great at messy,

24
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:22,990
unpredictable work: operating systems, databases, game logic,

25
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:23,769
your browser.

26
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,756
For raw math it's limited: about ten teraflops across all 96 cores,

27
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:32,660
roughly a hundred times less than a GPU's matrix units.

28
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:35,357
Its memory is twelve channels of DDR5,

29
00:01:35,357 --> 00:01:37,558
about 460 gigabytes per second.

30
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:39,800
Divide by our 8 gigabyte model,

31
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:42,317
and you get about 57 tokens per second,

32
00:01:42,317 --> 00:01:44,252
at best. Usable, but not fast.

33
00:01:45,100 --> 00:01:48,400
The GPU: instead of a few smart cores,

34
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:53,350
thousands of simple ones. The H100 has 16,896 CUDA cores,

35
00:01:53,350 --> 00:02:01,427
grouped into 132 streaming multiprocessors, plus 528 Tensor Cores built only for matrix math.

36
00:02:01,667 --> 00:02:03,943
Threads run in groups of 32 called warps,

37
00:02:03,943 --> 00:02:06,885
all executing the same instruction on different data.

38
00:02:06,885 --> 00:02:10,437
That's terrible for branchy code, where half the warp sits idle,

39
00:02:10,437 --> 00:02:12,935
but perfect for graphics and neural networks,

40
00:02:12,935 --> 00:02:15,877
where you do the same multiply-add millions of times.

41
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:20,326
The numbers: about 990 teraflops of 16-bit matrix math,

42
00:02:20,326 --> 00:02:25,473
80 gigabytes of high bandwidth memory at 3.35 terabytes per second,

43
00:02:25,473 --> 00:02:26,549
and 700 watts.

44
00:02:26,767 --> 00:02:33,690
Yardstick: 3.35 terabytes per second over 8 gigabytes is about 420 tokens per second.

45
00:02:33,690 --> 00:02:37,763
Seven times the CPU, and mostly because of memory,

46
00:02:37,763 --> 00:02:38,496
not math.

47
00:02:39,332 --> 00:02:42,058
The TPU, Google's Tensor Processing Unit.

48
00:02:42,058 --> 00:02:44,652
It isn't a general-purpose chip at all.

49
00:02:44,652 --> 00:02:48,109
It's built around one structure: the systolic array.

50
00:02:48,332 --> 00:02:52,519
Picture a 128 by 128 grid of multiply-accumulate cells.

51
00:02:52,519 --> 00:02:57,619
Weights sit inside the cells. Input numbers pulse in from the left,

52
00:02:57,619 --> 00:03:01,197
one step per clock, and partial sums flow down.

53
00:03:01,197 --> 00:03:05,231
Every value gets reused across a whole row or column,

54
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:07,819
without ever going back to memory.

55
00:03:08,065 --> 00:03:13,575
That's why it's efficient: almost no energy goes into fetching data.

56
00:03:13,575 --> 00:03:17,869
A TPU v5e chip delivers 197 teraflops of 16-bit math,

57
00:03:17,869 --> 00:03:22,407
with 16 gigabytes of memory at 819 gigabytes per second.

58
00:03:22,632 --> 00:03:26,038
Yardstick: about 100 tokens per second per chip.

59
00:03:26,038 --> 00:03:30,083
But TPUs are meant to be used in pods of up to 256 chips,

60
00:03:30,083 --> 00:03:32,141
wired directly to each other.

61
00:03:32,965 --> 00:03:35,228
The NPU, or Neural Processing Unit,

62
00:03:35,228 --> 00:03:38,396
is the AI block inside your phone or laptop chip.

63
00:03:38,396 --> 00:03:40,529
Apple calls it the Neural Engine.

64
00:03:40,529 --> 00:03:43,309
Qualcomm, Intel and AMD all have their own.

65
00:03:43,531 --> 00:03:46,667
It does the same kind of matrix math as a TPU,

66
00:03:46,667 --> 00:03:51,370
but tuned for low power, using 8-bit and 4-bit numbers instead of 16.

67
00:03:51,370 --> 00:03:56,823
The NPU in Qualcomm's Snapdragon X Elite does 45 trillion operations per second,

68
00:03:56,823 --> 00:03:58,186
on just a few watts.

69
00:03:58,431 --> 00:04:00,821
That's what runs face unlock, live captions,

70
00:04:00,821 --> 00:04:03,157
photo cleanup and background blur on calls,

71
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:06,634
without draining your battery or sending your data to the cloud.

72
00:04:06,865 --> 00:04:11,511
Yardstick: laptop memory runs at about 135 gigabytes per second,

73
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:15,939
so our 8 gigabyte model tops out around 17 tokens per second.

74
00:04:15,939 --> 00:04:20,657
Small models run on device. Big ones still go to the data center.

75
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:24,354
The LPU, Groq's Language Processing Unit,

76
00:04:24,354 --> 00:04:26,792
attacks the memory problem head on.

77
00:04:26,792 --> 00:04:29,091
It has no external memory at all.

78
00:04:29,091 --> 00:04:32,295
Each chip holds 230 megabytes of on-chip SRAM,

79
00:04:32,295 --> 00:04:34,803
readable at 80 terabytes per second:

80
00:04:34,803 --> 00:04:37,798
about 24 times the H100's memory bandwidth.

81
00:04:38,031 --> 00:04:40,217
The catch: 230 megabytes is tiny.

82
00:04:40,217 --> 00:04:44,192
A 70 billion parameter model needs about 300 of these chips,

83
00:04:44,192 --> 00:04:49,424
chained together, with a compiler scheduling every data movement ahead of time,

84
00:04:49,424 --> 00:04:51,014
down to the clock cycle.

85
00:04:51,231 --> 00:04:54,304
Cerebras takes the idea to the extreme.

86
00:04:54,304 --> 00:04:58,873
Instead of cutting a silicon wafer into hundreds of chips,

87
00:04:58,873 --> 00:05:01,788
it keeps the whole wafer as one chip:

88
00:05:01,788 --> 00:05:05,728
46,225 square millimeters, 4 trillion transistors,

89
00:05:05,728 --> 00:05:11,006
900,000 cores, and 44 gigabytes of SRAM at 21 petabytes per second.

90
00:05:11,231 --> 00:05:17,114
The payoff is speed. Cerebras has reported around 1,800 tokens per second on Llama

91
00:05:17,114 --> 00:05:20,557
3.1 8B, several times what a single GPU manages.

92
00:05:21,398 --> 00:05:23,762
The FPGA, or Field-Programmable Gate Array,

93
00:05:23,762 --> 00:05:27,171
is hardware you can rewire. It's a sea of small lookup tables,

94
00:05:27,171 --> 00:05:30,800
flip-flops and math blocks, with programmable wiring between them.

95
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:35,034
Load a new configuration file, and the same chip becomes a different circuit.

96
00:05:35,265 --> 00:05:38,590
That gives you custom hardware without building a chip:

97
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:43,305
sub-microsecond latency for high-frequency trading, network packet processing,

98
00:05:43,305 --> 00:05:46,327
and prototyping chips before they're manufactured.

99
00:05:46,327 --> 00:05:49,350
The cost: clock speeds of a few hundred megahertz,

100
00:05:49,350 --> 00:05:51,043
and much harder programming.

101
00:05:51,265 --> 00:05:55,030
The ASIC, or Application-Specific Integrated Circuit,

102
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:58,724
is the opposite: a chip that does exactly one thing,

103
00:05:58,724 --> 00:06:00,998
permanently. The TPU is an ASIC.

104
00:06:00,998 --> 00:06:06,610
So is a Bitcoin miner. An Antminer S21 computes 200 trillion hashes per second,

105
00:06:06,610 --> 00:06:10,021
something no CPU or GPU comes close to per watt.

106
00:06:10,265 --> 00:06:12,282
The trade-off is cost and time.

107
00:06:12,282 --> 00:06:17,291
A cutting-edge ASIC can cost tens of millions of dollars and years to design,

108
00:06:17,291 --> 00:06:20,609
and if the algorithm changes, the chip is obsolete.

109
00:06:21,431 --> 00:06:24,127
Last, the DPU, or Data Processing Unit.

110
00:06:24,127 --> 00:06:28,482
In a data center, a lot of CPU time goes to moving data around:

111
00:06:28,482 --> 00:06:32,560
networking, storage, encryption. A DPU takes that job over.

112
00:06:32,798 --> 00:06:38,676
NVIDIA's BlueField-3 combines a 400 gigabit per second network interface with 16 Arm cores

113
00:06:38,676 --> 00:06:41,810
and hardware engines for encryption and storage,

114
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:42,855
all on one card.

115
00:06:43,098 --> 00:06:47,799
In AI clusters, where thousands of GPUs constantly exchange data,

116
00:06:47,799 --> 00:06:50,186
the DPU keeps the network moving,

117
00:06:50,186 --> 00:06:53,730
so the CPUs and GPUs can focus on their own work.

118
00:06:54,565 --> 00:06:56,991
So here's the lineup by memory bandwidth,

119
00:06:56,991 --> 00:06:59,831
the number that decides how fast text comes out.

120
00:06:59,831 --> 00:07:02,553
From a laptop NPU at 135 gigabytes per second,

121
00:07:02,553 --> 00:07:05,334
to a Cerebras wafer at 21 petabytes per second.

122
00:07:05,334 --> 00:07:08,175
That's a range of over a hundred thousand times.

123
00:07:08,398 --> 00:07:11,367
And by job: the CPU for logic and control.

124
00:07:11,367 --> 00:07:15,822
The GPU for parallel math. The TPU and NPU for neural networks,

125
00:07:15,822 --> 00:07:18,155
in the data center and on device.

126
00:07:18,155 --> 00:07:22,185
The LPU and wafer-scale engines for very fast generation.

127
00:07:22,185 --> 00:07:26,214
FPGAs for custom low-latency circuits. ASICs for one job,

128
00:07:26,214 --> 00:07:29,113
done perfectly. And DPUs for moving data.

129
00:07:29,331 --> 00:07:32,909
Every one of them is a different answer to the same question:

130
00:07:32,909 --> 00:07:35,021
where do you spend your transistors?

131
00:07:35,021 --> 00:07:36,781
On smart control, on raw math,

132
00:07:36,781 --> 00:07:38,892
or on memory right next to the math.
